WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.979 --> 00:00:06.520
Once you learn the game of money, it is very
easy to make money.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.940
But as easy as it is to make money, it is
100 times easier to lose it all, if you make

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.559
one of these 10 mistakes I'll be talking about
today.

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:21.019
By the way, FYI, I have made every single
one of these mistakes I'm talking to you about

00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:22.019
today.

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:23.019
So it's purely coming to you from experience.

00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:26.550
So let's start off with the first mistake,
here we go.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.550
Wasted services.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:29.789
Let me explain to you what wasted services
means.

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:34.150
When you become an entrepreneur, and you start
making a little bit of money, what starts

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:39.701
happening is all these "messiah" consultants
show up and they say, "if you hire me, my

00:00:39.701 --> 00:00:43.109
expertise is knowing how to take your business
to the next level."

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.250
How much do you cost?

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.410
"I'm only $10,000 of consulting.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.510
You know, you need to pay me."

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.350
And you're just getting started, so you have
all this fear that you don't know what you're

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:55.039
doing, so you're kind of like, "well, I'll
pay this guy $10,000" but you only have $100,000.

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:56.600
Now 10% of your money is gone.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.840
Then this other person shows up.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.860
I know how to create the website that's going
to show you this.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:01.860
$3500.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.690
I know how to PR and get you into the newspaper
and the news, because if you pay me, $5,000.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.500
I know how to be your great.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.700
. . and the next thing you know, you just
lost $40,000.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.750
And you barely got the business started.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.660
So you've got to keep in mind that you don't
want to waste a lot of your money on these

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:20.860
"messiah" type of consultants that show up
to take all your money from you.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:21.860
Next, legal team.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.780
Let me explain to you why it's so important
to have a solid legal team.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.180
When I first started the company, and I started
my financial firm in October of '09, from

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.810
the moment I started, five minutes later,
I got sued.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.570
I got sued five minutes after starting the
company.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:39.110
Now, when we got sued, we got sued by a $400
billion company.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.530
What saved us is we had an incredible legal
team.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.130
Because if I would have gotten constantly
bullied, I would have eventually won the case,

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.040
but if they would have dragged it out two
years later, I would have run out of money.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.040
Because these guys were pretty heavy duty.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.450
So I got different types of attorneys.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.690
I got three different firms.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:02:01.090
I have general counsel, who handles my structural
type of my business, C-corp, S-Corp, how to

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.680
structure, stock options, you know, equity,
all this stuff.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:05.930
They do it.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:12.200
The other one is an industry legal attorney
that I hired.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.510
These guys are very expensive, the ones that
I have are 1200 bucks an hour, $800 and up.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.510
They're phenomenal.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:18.819
Then the other one is marketing.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:25.599
So if you got a sales side, if you got a commission
side, on making sure everything's being done

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:27.290
properly, that's legal side.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.150
Now, accounting is another one.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.999
Initially when I started, I had a regular
accountant who talked a big game, but every

00:02:32.999 --> 00:02:38.709
time I got a hold of him he was never available,
very annoying, very unprofessional, bad attitude,

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:39.749
had no clue what he was doing.

00:02:39.749 --> 00:02:43.210
He lost my paperwork all the time, over paid,
all this stuff.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.879
Finally I said, "You're fired."

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.989
I went and got a real accountant firm that
I hired.

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:54.129
At that time I think it was Cohen, I think
I got Cohen Resnick at that time.

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:57.079
They did a fantastic job because they're a
top 50 accounting firm in America.

00:02:57.079 --> 00:02:58.870
They did a phenomenal job.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:02:59.870
But they saved us.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.650
So we started doing accounting properly.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.110
It is very, very important for you to not
make mistakes with your legal side, your accounting

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.170
side, tax wise, pay all your taxes early.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.170
Don't wait.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:16.359
A lot of times people get this $100,000 check
or $50,000 check, and you're not getting W-2,

00:03:16.359 --> 00:03:18.129
so they don't take your taxes automatically.

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:23.189
You spend the 50, as if the 50's yours, instead
of spending the 50, as if the 25 is yours,

00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:24.999
and making sure you take care of your taxes.

00:03:24.999 --> 00:03:27.450
You don't take care of that part, you know
how many people are not in business today

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.400
simply because they have tax liens or they
didn't pay taxes.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.730
They lost all of this other stuff that hurt
their credit, they lost these things and people

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.579
aren't doing business with them anymore.

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:36.579
So legal.

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:38.079
Pay very close attention to that.

00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:40.359
Next, hiring resumes vs. believers.

00:03:40.359 --> 00:03:41.769
I made this mistake early.

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:48.041
I hired a resume that I brought on board and
all he cared about, all he cared about was

00:03:48.041 --> 00:03:51.279
the money he was going to make, the benefits
he was going to get, the American Express

00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:55.139
card he was going to get, because the former
company he was working for, he got all that

00:03:55.139 --> 00:03:56.909
stuff and he was expecting it from a startup.

00:03:56.909 --> 00:03:58.510
And he pressured me, every day.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.730
Well, you know, the former company gave me
this.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.309
The former company.

00:04:02.309 --> 00:04:04.919
. . Finally I realized, this guy's not a believer.

00:04:04.919 --> 00:04:06.349
This guy was a money guy.

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:07.919
All he wanted was to make sure he's safe.

00:04:07.919 --> 00:04:10.290
He didn't understand the value of a startup
company.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:13.203
So I asked him, this is not going to be a
fit.

00:04:13.203 --> 00:04:14.203
He walked away.

00:04:14.203 --> 00:04:15.203
He's doing good for himself.

00:04:15.203 --> 00:04:19.110
Then I brought a true believer who was part
of my board for five and a half years and

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.620
then eventually I brought him in because he's
no longer somebody that had just come at it

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:22.620
as business.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.190
He's going to make a lot of money but he came
in as a true believer.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.460
So don't just hire resumes.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.500
Hire believers.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.020
Next, residual checks.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.610
Let me explain to you what I mean.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:39.030
There's this notion, the cliche of one day
you're going to have so much money, where

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.850
these checks are piling in and they're coming
in and this money's going to come in and you're

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:49.150
at the beach and you're sitting there and
you know, it's so great, and you don't work

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.150
anymore.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.780
You're home all day long, sleep, wake up at
11:00 and you do whatever you want.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:54.960
This is great.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.600
This is the dream that's being sold, right?

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:04.310
I don't know if you realize kids, no matter
how old they become, they're still your kids.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:08.150
If you choose to have kids, you're a parent
for the rest of your life.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:13.460
If you build a business and you plan on growing
the business, it requires attention.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:18.400
No matter how big the business becomes, even
a $100 billion company needs attention.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.560
And maybe even more attention but at that
time you have some leaders and board and all

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.990
that stuff that's helping you take care of
the business.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.250
If you start living the dream too early, very,
very early, you're in deep trouble.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:33.980
When we started the company, and I had money
in place, and I put it all in the business,

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.560
I drove a GMC Acadia is what I drove.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.550
I drove a GMC Acadia.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.840
I sold my Z06, drove an Acadia and we were
doing our part and it was all good.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.180
And then slowly, we didn't have a house, we
rented an apartment, because we stayed tight.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.521
It wasn't about the lifestyle.

00:05:48.521 --> 00:05:50.920
You see the Lamborghini and all this other
stuff now.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.770
This is five, six, seven years later, after
having to put up with what we put up with.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.170
Then came the lifestyle.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:58.170
Next, liquid.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.170
Liquid.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.040
Let me tell you why liquid.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:07.070
You'll see a lot of people who all of a sudden
their net worth will go from zero to $17 million

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:09.990
on real estate, because the economy's doing
very well.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:10.990
And it's all good.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.930
So everybody says, "Oh my gosh, I got such
a genius.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:14.930
He's so great.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:15.930
He's so awesome.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:16.930
He's so this.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:24.140
I took over an office of somebody in Woodland
Hills who at one point was worth $400 million.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:30.280
His net worth went from $400 million to $20
million in two years, is what happened to

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.280
his net worth.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.280
You know why?

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.010
Because he was all real estate.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:35.930
And he wasn't liquid.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:38.910
And he thought real estate and mortgage, all
this stuff was going to constantly go up,

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.130
and up and up, and he was eventually going
to be a multi-billionaire.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.130
That's fine.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:48.480
You can go up there and put $100,000 on roulette
and say it's going to be #22 and if it comes

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.330
up, your $100,000 becomes $3.6 million.

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.810
There is a one is 36 chance that it's going
to happen.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.220
So if it happens to somebody, it's awesome.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.750
That happens to a lot of people that become
billionaires because they were at the right

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.490
place, right time, real estate, all this stuff.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:02.970
That does happen.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.710
But I don't bet my money on exceptions.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:08.680
I bet my money on predictability factors.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.290
Let me elaborate.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:14.900
Every 10 years, in the world, or in a country,
typically in America, some countries it's

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:20.270
more, but ours is generally 10 years, every
10 years a major crisis happens.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.170
Every 20 years, a massive crisis happens.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:26.110
But 10 years, a major crisis happens.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:29.990
When this crisis happens, let me tell you
who benefits from it.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.550
You know who benefits from it when a major
crisis happens?

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.560
Those who are liquid.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.560
Those who are liquid.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.380
You see a lot of people that say, "I never
have cash.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.470
I never have cash."

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.430
I love cash.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:41.920
I love cash.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:43.120
I LOVE cash.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.960
I was able to survive in '09, 2010 and 11,
when no insurance companies were giving contracts,

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.590
when insurance companies were losing money,
when AIG almost went out of business, when

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.220
all this stuff was happening because we stayed
tight.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:59.670
And that allowed us to be able to weather
the storm until the good times came, and now

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.110
we can play a little loose.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.110
But even now it's cash.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:03.110
You know why?

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:08.000
Because if 2008 was a major mortgage crisis
that happened, 2018, we could have another

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.000
crisis.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.000
What kind of crisis could it be?

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.000
I don't know.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.000
Oil?

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.000
War?

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.000
Something else?

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.000
But you've got to be prepared for it.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Okay, next.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.000
Profits over Value.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.250
A lot of times you'll see, I was advising
this one attorney who was running this law

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.230
firm and his law firm's been the same size
for 20 years.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:23.460
And he keeps asking me.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.410
What can I do to make my law firm bigger,
man?

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.640
It's so crazy, and all this other stuff.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:32.051
And then, his CFO is sitting there, the CFO
own 10% of the company, he owns 90% of the

00:08:32.051 --> 00:08:33.051
company.

00:08:33.051 --> 00:08:34.051
"I am really dedicated!"

00:08:34.051 --> 00:08:37.979
The CFO finally gets ticked off and says,
"Can I talk openly?" and he said, "Yes."

00:08:37.979 --> 00:08:40.729
The CFO said, "Look, I watch every single
month.

00:08:40.729 --> 00:08:43.930
Whatever profits we have, you take all of
it out of our account.

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:45.649
All of it out of your account.

00:08:45.649 --> 00:08:47.440
And there's nothing left in the company.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.389
So every time I want to invest or advertise,
you don't allow me to do it because you took

00:08:50.389 --> 00:08:51.950
all the profits.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.560
Because that person was only worried about
profits, not value.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.139
Value is a different thing.

00:08:57.139 --> 00:08:59.220
Value is what's the value of the company.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:02.990
And in order to think about the value of the
company, you got to do certain things.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:04.050
Profits you can take income all the time.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:07.170
There was many times I could have made a lot
more money, and just said, "give me the profit,

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.870
give me the profit, give me the profit."

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.949
I seek value more than I do profit.

00:09:10.949 --> 00:09:11.949
But that's a preference.

00:09:11.949 --> 00:09:13.290
Next, being too cheap.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:16.010
Too cheap could be investing, not into your
people.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.449
Saying, "Oh, I don't need to invest in my
people.

00:09:18.449 --> 00:09:19.850
They're good to go."

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:21.730
Not rewarding your people.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.620
Not investing into hiring good quality people.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.790
I'm not talking resumes, I'm talking believers.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:27.839
They require a lot more money as well.

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:29.930
But being too cheap, that can hurt you.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:33.339
Next is unrelated businesses.

00:09:33.339 --> 00:09:37.569
This is like imagine if Apple buys, I don't
know, give me an example.

00:09:37.569 --> 00:09:43.220
If Apple were to buy Toys R Us.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:44.220
For what?

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.899
Now Amazon bought, teamed up with Toys R Us.

00:09:46.899 --> 00:09:47.899
They did.

00:09:47.899 --> 00:09:48.899
Why?

00:09:48.899 --> 00:09:50.439
Because it's a storefront, it became a great
merge.

00:09:50.439 --> 00:09:52.249
Apple goes out and buys Beats.

00:09:52.249 --> 00:09:53.550
Why would Apple buy Beats?

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:56.560
Well, Beats links to iTunes, links to music.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.589
Okay, somewhat it makes sense.

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:00.050
Disney buys Make Studios.

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:01.410
Why would Disney buy Maker Studios?

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:02.920
It makes sense, okay?

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.309
You got some of these companies that are buying
certain people that makes sense.

00:10:06.309 --> 00:10:09.470
I made the mistake of investing into an unrelated
business.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:10.769
I bought a clothing line.

00:10:10.769 --> 00:10:11.980
For what?

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:12.980
Waste of money.

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.259
I lost a couple hundred thousand dollars and
even worse than the money I lost, I lost time.

00:10:17.259 --> 00:10:19.640
I put time into that thing that was wasted
time.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.870
I couldn't stand the fact that I did that.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:25.499
So unrelated business, zero interest in it.

00:10:25.499 --> 00:10:29.671
If it will benefit your business, somehow,
some way, consider it.

00:10:29.671 --> 00:10:31.459
If it doesn't, focus on your business.

00:10:31.459 --> 00:10:34.480
Next, active vs. passive investors.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.790
All these guys are all hungry about getting
an investor.

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:38.259
"I can't wait to get an investor."

00:10:38.259 --> 00:10:41.160
I had a guy that comes in and said, "I'll
give you $5 million."

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.509
This one guy that was interested in investing
into our company.

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:46.059
And I said, "What connections do you have?"

00:10:46.059 --> 00:10:47.059
Nothing.

00:10:47.059 --> 00:10:49.939
I just know that you're going to make a lot
of money and I know this company's going places

00:10:49.939 --> 00:10:51.129
and I'd like to own a piece of it.

00:10:51.129 --> 00:10:52.129
Here's $5 million."

00:10:52.129 --> 00:10:55.210
I said, "Why would I even consider your investment
here?

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:56.639
Because you're passive.

00:10:56.639 --> 00:10:57.870
Are you going to introduce me to anybody?"

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:58.870
No.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:10:59.870
"Do you know any relationships?"

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:00.870
No.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:01.870
Do you know anything?

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:02.870
"No.

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:03.870
But I'm going to give you $5 million."

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:04.870
I don't need the $5 million.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:06.050
I want active investors.

